OpenStax's own source data has several independent defects in this section,
none of them converter gaps -- the extractor faithfully reproduces what the
source MathML/HTML contains, and the source itself is wrong:

- Exercise 9's figure alt text contains a literal typo "Te=he" for "The".
- Exercise 11's figure alt text is missing the radius value: "a circle of
  radius with centers" should read "a circle of radius 1 with centers"
  (matching the "two circles of radius 2 and radius 1" already stated in
  the exercise's own prose).
- Exercise 22's Hint math span (<math>xdy-ydx=r^2 d\theta)</math>) itself
  includes the parenthetical's closing ")" and final "." as trailing
  MathML tokens, trapping the English parenthesis inside math mode.
  Moved both outside the $...$ span, where they belong grammatically.
- Exercise 65 (David and Sandra): the source repeats "(x,y)" as three
  separate, identical <math> spans instead of one. Collapsed to a single
  occurrence.
- Exercise 80's vector field is grouped incorrectly in the source MathML:
  <mrow>(cos(x^5))</mrow> minus (1/3)y^3, only afterward multiplied by i,
  leaves the first term with no i component at all -- not a valid vector
  expression. The unambiguous fix (matching the intended two-component
  field, and the parallel structure of every other \mathbf{F}(x,y) = (...)i
  + (...)j exercise in this file) is to bracket the whole first term before
  applying i: F(x,y) = (cos(x^5) - (1/3)y^3)i + (1/3)x^3 j.
- Exercise 87's exponent is encoded as <msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext><mo>-</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msup>
  -- "tan" followed by a minus sign followed by "1" subscripted by "x" --
  which has no sensible reading as MathML content; it's OpenStax's own
  mis-structured encoding of tan^{-1}(x) (inverse tangent), most likely
  from an authoring tool that meant to nest a superscript "-1" on "tan"
  but instead produced a subscript "1_x" concatenated after a literal
  minus. Corrected to \tan^{-1}x, consistent with this exercise's sibling
  (exercise 55 substitutes \sin x for the same "3y - e^{...}" integral
  pattern) and with this section's other inverse-tangent exercises
  (3, 45) which use \arctan.

